scarded tile is added but then part of the chow). Wait, no, the discarded tile is added to your hand, so you have 13 + 1 = 14 tiles. Then you form the chow, which uses three tiles (your two plus the discarded one). So your hand now has 14 - 3 = 11 tiles, plus the meld. But the total tiles in your hand (melds + remaining) should be 14. So after Chi, you have 11 tiles in hand plus the three in the chow, totaling 14. \\n\\nBut regardless, after performing a Chi, I need to see if my hand can form a winning hand. Since the game is in progress, I'm not sure if I can win immediately. The question is about which action to take now. \\n\\nThe valid actions are Chi T5, Chi T6, or Pass. The goal is to choose the best action. \\n\\nIf I Chi T5, forming the chow T4-T5-T6, then my hand would have that chow. The remaining tiles would be W1, W3, W6, W7, T1, T7, T9, F1, F4, J1, J3. That's 11 tiles. I need to form a winning hand with those. \\n\\nIf I Chi T6, forming T5-T6-T7, the remaining tiles would be W1, W3, W6, W7, T1, T4, T9, F1, F4, J1, J3. Also 11 tiles. \\n\\nNow, considering the remaining tiles, which scenario gives me a better chance to complete a winning hand. \\n\\nIn both cases, I have a chow in the T suit. Let's look for possible winning combinations. \\n\\nIn the first scenario (Chi T5), I have T4-5-6 chow. The remaining T tiles are T1, T7, T9. The W tiles are W1, W3, W6, W7. The honor tiles are F1, F4, J1, J3. \\n\\nPossible melds: \\n\\n- T4-5-6 (chow)\\n- T1 is a single, T7 and T9 are singles. Maybe a pair in T7 or T9? Not sure. \\n- W tiles: W1, W3, W6, W7. Maybe a chow of W3-4-5? But I don't have W4. Or W6-7-8? No W8. So not helpful. \\n- Honor tiles: F1, F4, J1, J3. Could form a pung if I have duplicates, but I don't. \\n\\nIn the second scenario (Chi T6), I have T5-6-7 chow. Remaining T tiles: T1, T4, T9. W tiles same. \\n\\nPossible melds: \\n\\n- T5-6-7 (chow)\\n- T1 and T4 and T9. Maybe a pair in T4? No. \\n- W tiles same as before. \\n\\nSo in both cases, 