 W3, I can later play W6 and W7 in subsequent turns to get rid of the non-terminals. But I need to pick one now. \\n\\nAlternatively, maybe playing W6. Let's see. If I play W6, then I have W1, W3, W7 left. Maybe those are less useful. \\n\\nWait, another angle: the hand has a meld of T4-5-6. If I can form another meld in T suit, that would help. For example, if I have T1 and T7, maybe forming a T1-2-3 and T7-8-9. But I don't have T2 or T8. So not possible. \\n\\nAlternatively, maybe I can keep T1 and T7 as part of a Two Terminal Chows pattern. But I need to have the chows. For example, if I have T1-2-3 and T7-8-9. But since I only have T1 and T7, and no T2, T3, T8, T9, it's not possible. \\n\\nHmm. Maybe I should focus on playing the W3, which is a middle tile, and not a terminal. That would help towards All Terminals and Honors. Let's go with that. So the action would be Play W3.\\n</think>\\n\\nEvaluation: Playing W3 to reduce non-terminal tiles and move closer to All Terminals and Honors pattern.\\nReason: The hand contains several non-terminal tiles (W3, W6, W7) which hinder the All Terminals and Honors (32 points) pattern. Removing W3 eliminates one non-terminal, preserving terminals (T1, T7, T9) and honors (F1, F4, J1, J3). This action maintains flexibility for future discards while progressing toward a high-value scoring pattern.\\nAnswer: Play W3\"}"}
