need it for a winning pattern. Similarly with T7. W1 is a terminal in the W suit. Maybe part of a pattern.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the best move is to discard a tile that's not part of any potential Pung or Chow. B4 is a single, so maybe safe to discard. Also, the unshown B4 has 4 left, so plenty available. Discarding B4 might not be bad.\n\nAnother angle: looking at the unshown tiles. The counts for B4 are 4, which is the same as other tiles. So no shortage. So discarding B4 is okay.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can look for which discard would not help others. If I discard T1, someone might Chi if they have T2 or T3. But since the previous player has a meld of T3-T4-T5, maybe they already have T3. So if I discard T1, they could Chi T1-T2-T3 if they have T2. But the unshown T2 has 3 left, so possible. So maybe T1 is risky to discard.\n\nSimilarly, W1: if someone has W2 or W3, they could Chi. The unshown W2 and W3 have 4 and 3 left. So possible. So maybe W1 is also risky.\n\nT7: if someone has T6 or T8, they could Chi. T6 is in my meld (T4-T5-T6), so they have T6. If I discard T7, someone could Chi T6-T7-T8 if they have T8. But T8 has 4 left, so possible.\n\nSo maybe the safest tile to discard is B4, as it's a single and not part of any obvious Chow. Also, B4's count is 4, so plenty available. So playing B4 would be a good choice.\n\nAlternatively, looking at the F1 and F4. Since I have two F1s, maybe keeping F1 is better. Discarding F4 or J1 or J3. J1 and J3 are single, but maybe part of a Pung. However, since they are single, discarding them might not be bad. But if I discard J1, and someone has J1, they could Peng. But there are 3 J1 left, so possible. So maybe not the best.\n\nSo the best option is to discard B4. That's the least useful tile for my hand and not part of any potential melds.\n</think>\n\nEvaluation: [The hand has a mix of terminals and middle tiles, with a meld of T4-T5-T6. B4 is a single tile not part of any potential Pung or Chow. Discarding B4 minimizes risk and maintains flexibility for forming higher-value patterns like All Terminals or All Terminals and Honors.]\nReason: [B4 is a middle tile with no immediate use in existing melds or potential Chows. Discarding it avoids giving others a chance to form Chows while preserving key terminals (T1, T7) and honors (F1, J1) for higher-point patterns.]\nAnswer: Play B4"}
