e another chow. For example, if I have W6, W7, and then a W8 to form a chow. But I don't have a W8. So this might not work unless I can get a W8.\n\nAlternatively, after forming [W3, W4, W5] and [W6, W6], then the remaining tiles would be W7, T1, T7, B9. Need to form a fourth meld. Maybe a pair of T7 and T7, but there's none left. Or a pair of B9 and B9, but only one left. So this seems unlikely.\n\nAnother possibility: Form a pair of B9 and B9. If I can draw a B9, then that's a pair. The unshown tiles have one B9 left. Then the remaining tiles would be W3, W5, W6, W6, W7, T1, T7. Need to form three melds. For example, [W5, W6, W7], [W3, W4, W5] (if I have a W4), and [T1, T2, T3] (if I have a T2). But I don't have those. So this might not be feasible.\n\nLooking back, the most straightforward way is to form the chow [W5, W6, W7] by keeping those tiles and hoping to draw a tile that can form a pair. The remaining tiles after that chow would be W3, T1, T7, B9. If I can draw a tile that pairs with one of these, like another T1, then that would work. The chance of drawing a T1 is possible since there's one left. Alternatively, if I can draw a B9, that would pair with the existing B9. But there's only one B9 left.\n\nSo, in terms of discarding, which tile would be safest? If I discard T1, I might be giving someone a chance to take it for a chi. But if I keep T1, it's part of the possible pair. Similarly, if I discard T7, someone might take it. But I don't have another T7. B9 is a terminal, so discarding it might not be useful for others unless they have a pair. W6 is part of the chow, so keeping it is better. W3 is a single tile, but if I discard it, someone might take it for a chi. For example, if someone has W2 and W4, they could take W3 for a chi. But looking at the melds, the next player has a W1, W2, W3. So if I discard W3, they might take it for a chi. But they already have that meld, so maybe they don't need it. However, the previous player has a meld of T3, T4, T